{
  "id": "SPEC-RELEASE-TRUST-010",
  "status": "draft",
  "owner_track": "release-security",
  "last_verified_revision": "289001ec",
  "depends_on": [
    "SPEC-RELEASE-DECISION-008",
    "SPEC-RELEASE-INTENT-MODEL-009"
  ],
  "purpose": "Define the Git-only source-trust and workspace-exclusion contract used by version-1 durable releases. It turns the approved source commit into freshly observed, non-durable trust evidence and prevents two Callisto release processes from validating or effecting the same workspace concurrently.",
  "scope": "Fresh Git observation, canonical workspace-root binding, clean detached-HEAD policy, local release locking, and re-observation rules. This does not define release policy, durable intent fields, graph preparation, provider effects, or a non-Git release mode.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Supporting branch, symbolic-HEAD, dirty-worktree, non-Git, or HermeticContent execution in version 1.",
    "Using a Git SHA as evidence of human authorship, signature verification, or forge authorization.",
    "Persisting a workspace path, lock handle, raw git output, remote URL, or arbitrary Git diagnostic in a durable intent, state, receipt, or artifact manifest.",
    "Making an advisory local lock substitute for re-observing Git state before effects."
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "Version-1 authorization starts from a caller-supplied workspace root, canonicalizes that root, discovers the Git repository containing it, and rejects a root that does not resolve to that repository's worktree root. The observed identity is the exact full CommitSha at HEAD; it is never supplied by a CLI flag, inferred from a branch name, or obtained from a remote ref. The private trust evidence binds the canonical root and observed commit together.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "Authorization accepts only a detached HEAD at the exact GitCommit SourceIdentity in the received ReleaseIntentV1. It rejects a symbolic HEAD even when it currently resolves to the same commit, an absent/unresolvable HEAD, a non-canonical or non-equal commit, and a shallow/incomplete checkout when the required commit or tag inspection cannot be performed. Equality uses CommitSha values, never prefix or raw-string comparison.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "Authorization requires a clean worktree before the capability is issued. 'Clean' means Git reports no staged, unstaged, unmerged, or untracked file under the repository worktree; ignored files do not make it dirty. A path outside the canonical root, an error while determining status, or an ambiguous repository layout fails closed. Diagnostics identify the policy condition and safe relative paths where available, but do not retain raw command output in durable data.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "A ReleaseWorkspaceLock is acquired after root/repository discovery and held from the final successful authorization observation until the validated capability is dropped after state/receipt persistence. Its lock identity is derived from the canonical workspace root using a stable collision-resistant digest and its lock file is outside the checkout in the platform application-state area (or a caller-selected operational directory). It is an exclusive OS-backed advisory lock, releases on process exit/crash, and reports contention with a stable structured error. The lock path and handle are private operational data, not serializable release data.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-005",
      "criterion": "The release executor re-observes the same canonical root, detached HEAD, clean-worktree status, and exact approved commit while holding ReleaseWorkspaceLock immediately before every remote or VCS effect. A change after authorization blocks the next effect; the lock only coordinates cooperating Callisto processes and never makes external writers safe by assumption.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-006",
      "criterion": "The trust API is a read-only callisto-vcs surface returning closed, typed GitCommitTrustEvidence and ReleaseWorkspaceLock values. It does not depend on callisto-graph, callisto-cli, forge SDKs, or registry clients. Its errors derive miette::Diagnostic with stable codes and remediation, and its durable-facing output contains only validated typed identities rather than shell text.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-007",
      "criterion": "Tests cover non-Git roots, nested/non-root invocation, symbolic HEAD, dirty tracked and untracked worktrees, commit mismatch, lock contention, lock release after drop, and a mutation between authorization and an effect-time recheck. Tests assert that each failure occurs before an effect adapter is invoked.",
      "is_error_case": true
    }
  ],
  "implementation_notes": [
    "GitAccess/ShellGit should expose structured observation rather than making graph parse porcelain or command text. The existing CommitSha type is the equality boundary.",
    "Use a maintained cross-platform file-lock crate only if it provides process-lifetime exclusive locking with explicit acquisition errors. Keep the dependency in callisto-vcs or a lower permitted layer; do not introduce Tokio or Rayon for this ordered, lock-held path.",
    "A future closed-root byte observer may add HermeticContent under a separate schema/profile version. It must not weaken the GitCommit behavior specified here."
  ],
  "reasoning": "A Git commit only becomes a release trust boundary when the executor proves it is operating at the approved checkout and prevents its own concurrent release processes from racing. A local lock improves coordination; mandatory fresh observation preserves correctness when another tool changes the workspace."
}

{
  "id": "SPEC-RELEASE-CAPABILITY-011",
  "status": "draft",
  "owner_track": "release-security",
  "last_verified_revision": "289001ec",
  "depends_on": [
    "SPEC-RELEASE-DECISION-008",
    "SPEC-RELEASE-INTENT-MODEL-009",
    "SPEC-RELEASE-TRUST-010"
  ],
  "purpose": "Define fresh graph authorization as the only conversion from a durable approved intent into exact, private execution inputs. The resulting capability is deliberately non-transferable and is the authority an effect executor needs in addition to ApplyPermit.",
  "scope": "Root-bound fresh semantic observation, exact intent comparison, release-DAG construction, prepared-operation ownership, and the public/private API boundary in callisto-graph. This spec does not define provider network protocols, durable state/receipts, artifact download, or CLI/workflow command syntax.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Replanning a release from current configuration or silently substituting a current selection for an approved release decision.",
    "Treating PublishPlan, PublishReport, PLAN_JSON, a registry URL supplied at execution time, a recomputed tag, or a mutable config object as effect authority.",
    "Serializing, cloning, caching, or transmitting prepared endpoints, filesystem paths, lock handles, parsed provider clients, credentials, or ValidatedReleaseIntent itself.",
    "Parallel effects or arbitrary operation reordering."
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "callisto-graph creates a release observation only from a canonical workspace root while holding the GitCommitTrustEvidence and ReleaseWorkspaceLock required by SPEC-RELEASE-TRUST-010. It re-runs the normal configuration resolution, package discovery, linked-group/cascade/prerelease policy resolution, and dependency analysis from disk; it never trusts a caller-provided workspace, selection, package list, endpoint, source SHA, or previously cached Workspace interior state as fresh evidence.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "Validation compares the received ReleaseIntentV1 exactly against the newly observed typed ReleaseDecisionV1 and typed semantic-input snapshot from SPEC-RELEASE-INTENT-MODEL-009. It rejects any difference in source identity, selected/linked-group-expanded roster, target version, prerelease/group/cascade policy, package role/path identity, relevant dependency edges, registry binding identity, tag/forge policy, declared artifact slots, operation identity, prerequisite set, or canonical operation order. It must not compare raw configuration bytes, Display/Debug strings, generic kind/fingerprint pairs, or credential-bearing endpoint text.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "The operation graph is constructed from the approved typed operation identities and explicit prerequisites. It is a DAG by validation; its canonical order is a stable Kahn topological order that always selects the lexicographically smallest ready ReleaseOperationId. A cycle, a missing prerequisite, a duplicate identity, or an operation outside the approved roster is a structured authorization failure. Dependency ordering is never inferred from enum declaration order or hash-map iteration.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "On an exact match, graph returns ValidatedReleaseIntent. This type is defined in callisto-graph, is non-Clone and non-Serialize/non-Deserialize, has no public constructor, and keeps its fields private. It owns the matching ReleaseIntentV1, GitCommitTrustEvidence, ReleaseWorkspaceLock, canonical root, and a complete BTreeMap keyed by ReleaseOperationId of private PreparedOperation values. It may expose the durable intent for inspection but exposes no raw prepared operation value or authority to alter it.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-005",
      "criterion": "Each PreparedOperation is created during the same successful fresh observation and contains exactly the effect inputs for its operation: registry operations retain the resolved package directory, exact qualified package/version, parsed and policy-checked endpoint, typed RegistryBindingId, and any source-artifact reference; tag operations retain the exact TagName, approved target CommitSha, and annotation policy/message; forge/binary operations retain the exact intent-bound artifact-slot and tag/target binding. Endpoint preparation uses a standards-compliant URL parser, rejects userinfo, query, and fragment components, normalizes the approved binding before its digest is compared, and never writes raw endpoint text to any durable type or diagnostic.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-006",
      "criterion": "Effect-dispatch APIs accept a ValidatedReleaseIntent reference plus ApplyPermit and select prepared operations only by exact ReleaseOperationId. They neither accept PublishPlan nor consult mutable workspace configuration to reconstruct package paths, endpoints, tags, versions, or prerequisites. A capability can be used only while its lock/trust evidence remains valid; the executor invokes the effect-time recheck from SPEC-RELEASE-TRUST-010 before dispatching each operation.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-007",
      "criterion": "Validation failures are stable GraphError variants with miette diagnostics that identify the safe semantic field or operation identity that changed. They do not include credentials, endpoint text, raw configuration, arbitrary provider output, or a debug rendering of a prepared operation. A mismatch yields no capability and no effect adapter invocation.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-008",
      "criterion": "Tests use independently constructed workspaces and fake effect dispatchers to prove rejection for changed manifest/config/policy/selection/DAG/source/registry-binding/artifact-slot inputs, endpoint credentials/query/fragments, duplicate or cyclic operations, and source substitution. Tests prove a valid intent retains prepared inputs from the observed workspace and that every rejected validation path makes zero adapter calls; they also prove stable topological order across differently inserted equivalent input collections.",
      "is_error_case": true
    }
  ],
  "implementation_notes": [
    "Keep durable semantic types in callisto-model and discovery/preparation in callisto-graph to preserve the license/dependency direction. callisto-model must not depend on callisto-graph.",
    "Use BTreeMap/BTreeSet and explicit Ord implementations for durable and scheduling order. Do not use PackageId::matches for release-operation identity: it intentionally supports a bare-name wildcard and is not an exact authorization predicate.",
    "Workspace currently contains interior caches. Fresh authorization should construct or invalidate an observation boundary rather than claiming a pre-existing cached graph is current."
  ],
  "reasoning": "The durable intent records what was approved. It is intentionally not sufficient to perform effects: filesystem locations, endpoints, and clients are mutable operational inputs. A graph-private, freshly observed capability bridges that gap without serializing secrets or allowing an old plan path to regain authority."
}

